The intersection of two enumerated neighbourhoods equals a third if and only if either the first extends the second and the third decodes to the first address, or the second extends the first and the third decodes to the second address. The intersection of two neighbourhoods of finite addresses is the more specific one when they are nested, and otherwise no enumerated neighbourhood, all of which are nonempty, equals the empty intersection.

Unfold the enumeration. The nested-or-disjoint property gives three cases. If the first neighbourhood is contained in the second, the corresponding prefix characteristic is one and the intersection equals the first neighbourhood, so equality with the third is agreement of the decoded lists by injectivity of neighbourhoods. The other disjunct gives mutual inclusion by the prefix criterion. The symmetric case is the same with the roles exchanged. If the intersection is empty, neither prefix characteristic is one, because inclusion would put a point of a nonempty neighbourhood into the empty intersection, and equality with the third fails because that neighbourhood is nonempty.
